NAME
    head - print the first lines of a file

SYNOPSIS
    head [-N] [FILE]
    head [FILE]

DESCRIPTION
    Prints the first 10 lines of FILE (default: stdin). With -N
    (e.g. -5), prints the first N lines.

    Inside a `|>` structured pipeline, head silently consumes the
    --advjson flag and counts JSONL records the same way it counts
    text lines — newlines work either way.

EXAMPLES
    head /etc/passwd
    head -1 /etc/passwd                 # first line only
    ps |> sort -k pid |> head -1        # process with lowest pid
    cat huge.log | head -50

EXIT STATUS
    0   lines were printed (or FILE was shorter than N)
    1   FILE could not be opened

SEE ALSO
    tail(1), cat(1), grep(1)
